Myofascial Pain Syndrome
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Taut muscle bands, Tender myofascial trigger points, Predictable referred pain patterns

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-034
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of unremitting neck and shoulder pain with severe referred headache, rendering the patient unable to sleep or concentrate.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient in severe distress due to an intractable, locking muscular pain radiating into the head, causing extreme fatigue, panic, and an inability to function. Urgent assessment was strictly required to differentiate severe myofascial trigger point crisis from acute cervical radiculopathy, meningitis, or subarachnoid hemorrhage, and to provide immediate, potent pharmacological and mechanical relief prior to daytime services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a rock-hard knot in my shoulder that is radiating a blinding, tight band of pain right up over my skull; nothing touches the pain and I haven't slept in two days."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe flare of chronic myofascial pain syndrome. Timeline: Long-standing history of deep, aching pain in the upper back and neck; rapidly escalated over the past 48 hours following a period of intense emotional stress and sustained poor posture at a computer workstation. Currently reports the presence of palpable, taut, ropy bands within the bilateral upper trapezius and levator scapulae muscles. Patient describes exquisite, localized tenderness at specific hyperirritable nodules. Crucially, the patient reports a predictable referred pain pattern: pushing on the right trapezius reliably elicits a radiating pain projecting over the occiput and temporal region (tension-type headache).
Functional Status: Severely functionally impaired by pain and cognitive fatigue; unable to work or drive safely.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound sleep deprivation and acute psychological distress secondary to unremitting pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 144/90 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, highly anxious, massaging the right trapezius continuously, holding the head rigidly.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is completely normal: 5/5 motor strength in upper limbs, intact sensation to light touch/pinprick globally, and symmetrical 2+ reflexes. Negative Kernig/Brudzinski signs. No photophobia. Inspection and palpation reveal multiple palpable, taut, ropy bands within the upper trapezius muscle bellies bilaterally. Exquisite localized tenderness noted at specific myofascial trigger points. Sustained manual compression of the right superior trapezius trigger point reliably reproduces the patient's exact referred temporal headache. A localized twitch response (involuntary transient muscle contraction) is provoked by snapping palpation of the trigger point. Active and passive range of motion of the cervical spine is physically restricted by the taut muscle bands, but joint mechanics are intact.
Environmental Observations: Work laptop and poorly configured home office setup visible in the living room.
Medication Reconciliation: Maxed out on Paracetamol, Ibuprofen, and over-the-counter Codeine with negligible relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Myofascial Pain Syndrome Crisis with Cervicogenic Headache - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Subarachnoid Hemorrhage - Ruled out by the gradual mechanical onset over 48 hours (rather than a sudden "thunderclap"), normal neurological exam, and the distinct, reproducible musculoskeletal trigger for the headache.

Cervical Radiculopathy - Ruled out clinically by the entirely normal motor, sensory, and reflex examination of the upper extremities, and absence of electric, shooting pain into the dermatomes of the arm/hand.

Meningitis - Ruled out by the absence of fever, negative meningeal signs, and lack of systemic toxicity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of severe distress driven by an acute myofascial pain crisis, characterized by intense, locked trigger points referring severe pain into the cranium. The critical clinical task was to rapidly and definitively rule out life-threatening central nervous system pathology (SAH, meningitis) that can mimic severe cervicocranial pain. Clinical examination firmly established the musculoskeletal and localized nature of the pathology, mapped exactly to active myofascial trigger points. Postponing evaluation would have subjected the patient to escalating panic, severe unmanaged pain, and dangerous sleep deprivation. Urgent after-hours assessment provided crucial diagnostic reassurance, excluded neurological emergencies, and safely initiated higher-tier pharmacological muscle relaxants to break the pain-spasm cycle.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT. Prescribed oral Diazepam 2mg BD PRN for up to 3 days to explicitly down-regulate central nervous system arousal and break the taut muscular bands.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Apply a hot, moist towel strictly over the tight shoulder muscles for 20 minutes before attempting to sleep or work. Consequence: Increased local vascular perfusion immediately softens the rigid, ischemic muscle knots, rapidly dissolving the referred headache (positive reinforcement).
Action: Set a strict 30-minute timer when using the computer, explicitly to stand up and perform three slow, deep shoulder shrugs and neck retractions. Consequence: Prevention of the static, isometric muscle overload that causes the trigger points to form and lock in the first place (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for trigger point release therapy (dry needling/massage) and postural re-education.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5-7 days if symptoms persist to discuss long-term management strategies for chronic myofascial pain.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden onset of an explosive "worst headache of your life," new fevers, vomiting, or sudden weakness or numbness in the arms or face.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, focusing on the muscular system. Persistent headaches unresponsive to therapy require further investigation by a regular GP. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute locking spasms and headache will ease over 48 hours with the muscle relaxants and heat. However, the trigger points will immediately return if the poor computer posture and stress levels are not addressed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you develop a high fever, if the headache suddenly becomes explosive and blinding, or if you develop numbness in your arms.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Prolonged stress and poor posture have caused specific bands of muscle in your shoulders to cramp intensely and lock up into "trigger points." These knots are so tight they strangle their own blood supply, radiating severe pain signals up into your head. The injection reduces the chemical pain, while the short course of Valium acts to chemically unlock the muscle fibers.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking codeine; it does not fix muscle spasms and causes rebound headaches. Use the hot towel aggressively to melt the tension. You must break up your computer time to stop the muscles from locking again.

